## 1. Executive Summary

The Property ROI Analysis Tool is a seller-facing planning system whose central product question is: **“What should this seller spend money on, in what order, and why?”** The system must intake property information, seller constraints, market context, repair or improvement opportunities, and governed source data to produce a prioritized pre-sale action plan. The MVP should remain the smallest high-value product that can rank, explain, and sequence seller actions without overpromising valuation precision, model certainty, or operational feasibility.[1]

Technical Specification V4 replaces Technical Specification V3 as the current implementation-control document. V4 preserves the core architecture, sequencing, governance, and legal posture from V3, while incorporating the newer regression-governance decisions from Decisions Log V12. The most important V4 updates are: **corrected ATTOM sale-date mapping**, **Greenville corrected comparable-sales fixture treatment**, **explicit distinction between pipeline validation and production calibration**, **versioned model-registry artifacts**, **model-registry validation and promotion gates**, and **recommendation-engine refusal of unapproved coefficient artifacts**.[4] [5] [6] [7]

> **V4 implementation posture:** The project has advanced from “regression input data blocked” to “Greenville smoke pipeline runnable and structurally validated,” but it is still **not ready to wire regression coefficients into seller-facing recommendations**. The Greenville v0.1 artifact is diagnostic-only and must remain unavailable to recommendation logic unless a future decision record explicitly approves a later artifact and the artifact itself is marked `approved_for_recommendations: true`.[4] [5]

