## 3. Product and Technical Scope

The MVP must help sellers choose among candidate repairs, listing-preparation tasks, and pre-sale improvements by estimating expected seller value, feasibility, timing risk, and sequencing order. The tool should explain recommendations in plain language and avoid unsupported precision.

| Scope Area | MVP Treatment | Later Phase Treatment | Rejected / Deferred Treatment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Seller action prioritization | Required | Refine with observed outcomes and richer calibration | Do not ship generic home-improvement advice without seller-specific prioritization |
| ROI recommendation engine | Required with bounded static assumptions, confidence labels, and refusal of unapproved models | Improve through approved market calibration, observed outcomes, and richer property attributes | Do not use diagnostic regression coefficients in seller-facing recommendations |
| Scarcity-aware sequencing | Required, but coding blocked until seed gates close | Add contractor availability, live scheduling, and more dynamic constraints | Do not rely on generic fallback timing for sequencer-required items |
| Professional staging anomaly | Required | Add more anomaly rules for source quality, extreme timeline compression, and dependency conflicts | Do not silently suppress staging solely because timing is short |
| Accessibility subtype handling | Required | Add richer subtypes if supported by source evidence and observed use | Do not use one broad accessibility timing range for all cases |
| Regression calibration | Required as governed analysis path, not as blind coefficient generation | Add empirical recalibration by market and property type after promotion gates | Do not estimate or consume coefficients for attributes absent from valid training data |
| Model registry and promotion governance | Required | Promote approved models into database-backed registry if warranted | Do not hardcode coefficients or load artifacts with `approved_for_recommendations: false` |
| Legal/compliance review | Required before public release | Continue review as jurisdictions and claim language expand | Do not present recommendations as legal, appraisal, tax, or contractor guarantees |

## 4. System Architecture

V4 adopts the **portable modular architecture** established in V3. The application should be structured around clear internal service boundaries so hosting, storage, scheduled jobs, external integrations, model artifacts, and recommendation logic can be swapped or hardened without rewriting the core product workflow.[2]

The architecture must include a frontend application, backend application services, relational persistence, private object storage, external data adapters, scheduled ingestion jobs, static seed-data management, model-artifact governance, recommendation-engine services, report-generation services, and governance/audit logging.

| Layer | Responsibility | MVP Requirement |
|---|---|---|
| Frontend | Collect seller/property inputs and present prioritized action plans | Mobile-responsive web interface with clear input guidance and explanation-heavy outputs |
| Backend API | Validate inputs, orchestrate data retrieval, load approved artifacts, run recommendation logic, and persist sessions | Server-side API with authenticated access if user accounts are included in MVP release |
| Recommendation Engine | Score, rank, sequence, constrain, and explain seller actions | Deterministic, traceable, seed-table-driven rules plus only approved calibration artifacts |
| Model Artifact Layer | Load, validate, and gate regression/model artifacts | Must refuse diagnostic, candidate, retired, rejected, structurally invalid, or unapproved artifacts |
| Data Layer | Store users, properties, analysis runs, recommendations, seed tables, model metadata, traces, and source metadata | Relational database with versioned seed data and audit fields |
| Object Storage | Store generated reports, uploaded files, downloaded static datasets, fixtures, model artifacts, and workpapers | Private storage with backend-generated short-lived signed access URLs |
| Integration Layer | Encapsulate ATTOM, Census, ACS, HUD, FHFA, Redfin, or similar external data sources | Adapter pattern with source-status logging, corrected field mapping, and graceful degradation |
| Scheduled Jobs | Refresh static datasets and market source pulls | Provider-neutral scheduler; implementation provider deferred |
| Governance Layer | Track source provenance, decisions, seed versions, model versions, confidence status, and promotion approvals | Required for auditability and defensibility |

## 5. Provider-Neutral Deployment Architecture

Technical Specification V2 contained Azure-specific implementation details. Decisions Log V10 superseded that posture by approving a portable architecture. Azure may remain an acceptable deployment candidate, but V4 must not require Azure-only services unless a future decision explicitly reintroduces that constraint.[2]

| Capability | V2 Azure-Centered Example | V4 Provider-Neutral Requirement |
|---|---|---|
| Static/object storage | Azure Blob Storage with SAS URLs | Private object storage with backend-generated short-lived signed URLs; Azure Blob, Supabase Storage, S3-compatible storage, or equivalent are acceptable candidates |
| Secret management | Azure Key Vault | Server-side secrets; environment variables acceptable for prototype, managed vault preferred for production hardening |
| Scheduled ingestion | Azure Functions Timer Trigger | Weekly scheduled ingestion through GitHub Actions cron, managed cron, Azure Functions, server scheduler, or equivalent provider-neutral mechanism |
| Hosting | Azure-oriented deployment assumptions | Web host and backend host may be selected based on cost, portability, operational burden, and integration fit |
| External data adapters | Service-specific implementation assumptions | Adapter interfaces must isolate provider SDKs, API-specific response formats, and source-path mappings |
| Model artifacts | Not previously explicit | Store registry artifacts in controlled file paths for development; later promote approved model metadata and coefficients into database tables if required |

## 6. Frontend Architecture

The frontend should be a responsive web application optimized for seller decision-making. The interface must make tradeoffs visible: estimated value impact, expected cost, timing constraints, dependency ordering, confidence level, and explanation. The MVP should avoid excessive configuration screens and should prioritize a guided workflow that collects only the minimum information needed to answer the core seller question.[1]

The frontend must not expose regression coefficients, raw model artifacts, API keys, or internal diagnostics. If model-calibrated estimates are later approved for seller-facing use, the frontend should display confidence-qualified output and plain-language reasoning rather than model internals.

| Frontend Area | Requirement | UX Risk |
|---|---|---|
| Property intake | Capture address or property identifier, property facts, seller timeline, budget, and listing goals | Excessive intake burden could reduce completion |
| Improvement intake | Support selected known repairs, observed property issues, optional photos or inspection notes if included in scope | Free-text repairs may create taxonomy ambiguity |
| Recommendation review | Present prioritized plan with “do first,” “do if budget allows,” “urgent,” “constrained,” and “not recommended” style groupings | Seller may misread confidence as guarantee |
| Timeline view | Show sequenced actions with lead-time warnings and dependency blockers | Sequencing without source-quality labels may appear more certain than it is |
| Report output | Provide explanation-rich summary that can be shared or reviewed later | Report language requires legal/UX review before public release |
| Mobile behavior | Support mobile-first use cases for quick review and data entry | Complex tables and charts must degrade gracefully |
| Model-confidence copy | Explain whether value logic is static, diagnostic, candidate, or approved calibration where relevant | Overexposure of model jargon may confuse sellers; underexposure may overstate certainty |

## 7. Backend Architecture

The backend should enforce validation, authorization, orchestration, source isolation, model-artifact gating, and traceability. Recommendation logic should not live directly in UI components. External data calls should pass through integration adapters, and recommendation output should preserve enough trace data to explain why each action was recommended, delayed, warned, or rejected.

| Backend Service | Responsibility | MVP Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Property Service | Normalize property profile, owner-provided facts, and external property attributes | ATTOM enrichment should be audited before relying on enriched attributes |
| Market Data Service | Fetch or read market indicators and local comps where available | Must track source date, source confidence, fallback status, and fixture versus production-dataset status |
| ATTOM Adapter / Export Mapper | Retrieve and normalize ATTOM property and comparable-sales fields | Must implement approved `sale_date` fallback mapping in the mapper, not in regression logic |
| Model Artifact Loader | Load model-registry artifacts and enforce structural and promotion requirements | Must refuse unapproved artifacts and return safe errors to recommendation services |
| Recommendation Service | Generate candidate actions, score ROI, apply constraints, sequence actions, and produce explanations | Must be deterministic and traceable in MVP; may use only approved coefficient artifacts |
| Sequencer Service | Apply duration, lead-time, dependency, budget, and target-list-date constraints | Coding blocked until dependency and source-QA gates close |
| Anomaly Service | Attach warnings such as staging under 21 days | Separate anomaly handling from ranking and scoring |
| Report Service | Assemble seller-facing report and traceable internal output | Public copy requires legal/UX review |
| Source Governance Service | Track static and external source metadata, seed versions, confidence labels, and decision references | Required before implementation readiness |
| Model Governance Service | Track model status, validation report, promotion readiness, approval decision, and retirement state | Required before coefficients can influence seller-facing output |

