### 10.1 ATTOM `sale_date` Fallback Mapping

The ATTOM comparable-sales export must populate the application-level `sale_date` field from the actual ATTOM sale snapshot response fields in the following approved order. This logic belongs in the pull/export mapper and must not be repaired downstream inside regression scripts.[4]

| Mapping Priority | ATTOM Response Path | V4 Treatment |
|---:|---|---|
| 1 | `sale.saleTransDate` | Approved primary field |
| 2 | `sale.salesearchdate` | Approved fallback 1 |
| 3 | `sale.amount.salerecdate` | Approved fallback 2 |
| Rejected | `sale.amount.saletransdate` | Defective mapping; must not be used as the expected source path |

Every regression fixture or model artifact that relies on ATTOM comparable-sales exports must record the source dataset path, training or filter window, row counts, nonblank `sale_date` coverage, parseable `sale_date` coverage, and date-window coverage. The export process must validate nonblank and parseable `sale_date` coverage before running regression.[4]

## 11. Recommendation Engine Overview

The recommendation engine should be deterministic, explainable, and seed-table-driven in MVP. It should combine seller inputs, property facts, market context, cost assumptions, value-impact estimates, duration and lead-time assumptions, dependency constraints, approved calibration artifacts, and anomaly rules to produce a prioritized action plan.

| Engine Step | Description | Required Trace Output |
|---|---|---|
| Input normalization | Convert property, seller, timeline, and candidate improvement data into canonical fields | Input snapshot and normalization warnings |
| Candidate generation | Identify eligible repair/improvement actions from catalog and property signals | Candidate IDs and trigger reasons |
| Eligibility filtering | Remove actions outside scope, unsupported by data, or contradicted by seller constraints | Filter reason and data source |
| Model artifact selection | Select only approved, structurally valid, market-appropriate coefficient artifacts where needed | Model ID, version, market, status, approval flag, validation report reference |
| ROI scoring | Estimate value contribution, cost, net benefit, and confidence | Coefficient set or seed assumption, source status, confidence label |
| Timeline feasibility | Apply duration, lead-time, target-list-date, and schedule-window constraints | Duration rule ID and feasibility status |
| Dependency sequencing | Order actions using dependency rules and critical-path flags | Dependency rule IDs and sequencing rationale |
| Anomaly detection | Attach warnings that modify explanation or urgency without necessarily suppressing action | Anomaly ID and message template |
| Output assembly | Produce seller-facing recommendation plan and internal trace | Ranked list, categories, reasons, warnings, and trace IDs |

The recommendation engine must fail closed on model-artifact approval. If a model is diagnostic-only, candidate, retired, rejected, structurally invalid, missing required fields, or marked `approved_for_recommendations: false`, the engine must refuse to consume it and must either fall back to approved static assumptions or return a controlled “model unavailable” status depending on the affected feature.[4] [5]

## 12. ROI, Hedonic, Regression Calibration, and Model Registry Requirements

Regression calibration must not estimate or consume coefficients for attributes missing from the training data. The first regression pass should use only fields with sufficient observed comp-level coverage. Attributes without reliable comp-level data must remain governed placeholder coefficients, owner-reviewed static assumptions, or qualitative recommendation-engine signals until a valid source exists.[2]

V4 adds a stricter model lifecycle. The Greenville smoke regression has passed for pipeline validation only, not for production calibration. A runnable pipeline and structurally valid artifact are necessary but insufficient conditions for recommendation use.[4] [5] [6]

| Calibration State | Meaning | Product May Use for Seller-Facing Recommendations? |
|---|---|---:|
| Pipeline-runnable | Script can load data, clean rows, train baseline model, and output diagnostics | No |
| Diagnostic model | Model exists to evaluate data quality, feature weakness, target choice, and workflow correctness | No |
| Candidate model | Artifact passed structural validation and is under promotion-readiness review | No |
| Approved recommendation model | Artifact passed validation, promotion review, manual approval, and has `approved_for_recommendations: true` | Yes |
| Retired or rejected model | Artifact must not be selected | No |

| Calibration Area | V4 Requirement | Risk if Ignored |
|---|---|---|
| Comp-level fields | Use only fields actually present and sufficiently populated in comp data | False precision and unstable coefficients |
| Richer ATTOM fields | Audit enriched endpoint availability before relying on condition, quality, view, effective year built, or similar attributes | Model may depend on unavailable data |
| Placeholder coefficients | Label separately from empirical coefficients | Users and developers may treat assumptions as facts |
| Diagnostic coefficients | Store only in model registry or diagnostics with explicit nonapproval status | Weak models may influence recommendations |
| Coefficient bounds | Define reasonableness limits and review process | Outlier results may distort rankings |
| Versioning | Store model version, source, calibration date, training window, status, and approval flag | Recommendations become unreproducible |
| QA tests | Test sign, magnitude, missingness, sensitivity, validation status, and refusal behavior | Engine may recommend counterintuitive or unsafe priorities |

### 12.1 Model Registry Contract

Each model-registry artifact should be a self-contained JSON document that includes enough metadata to determine how the model was trained, what data it used, whether it is approved, and whether the application is allowed to load it.[5]

| Field Group | Required Content | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Identity | `model_id`, `model_version`, `market`, `model_type`, `target` | Prevents ambiguous coefficient usage |
| Training scope | `training_window`, `source_dataset_path`, `modeling_dataset_path`, row counts | Ensures reproducibility and date-window accountability |
| Metrics | `train_r2`, `test_r2`, MAE, RMSE, and future cross-validation metrics | Supports promotion review |
| Model parameters | `intercept`, `coefficients`, `feature_names`, `input_features` | Allows deterministic scoring |
| Preprocessing | Scaling, one-hot encoding, target transform, missing-value handling | Prevents misuse of coefficients without required transformations |
| Governance | `status`, `approved_for_recommendations`, `created_at`, approval notes | Enforces product-safety controls |

The product must not interpret raw coefficient values unless the artifact also specifies preprocessing assumptions. This is especially important because the current Greenville Ridge model uses standardized numeric features; standardized coefficients are not raw-dollar or raw-square-foot effects.[6]

### 12.2 Approved Model Status Values

| Status | Meaning | Product May Use? |
|---|---|---:|
| `diagnostic_only` | Artifact exists for pipeline validation, debugging, or model exploration | No |
| `candidate` | Artifact has passed structural validation and is under promotion review | No |
| `approved` | Artifact has passed review and is explicitly approved for recommendation use | Yes, only if `approved_for_recommendations: true` |
| `retired` | Artifact was formerly used or considered but must no longer be selected | No |
| `rejected` | Artifact failed review and should not be promoted | No |

The application must enforce both conditions for product consumption: `status == "approved"` and `approved_for_recommendations == true`. This dual condition reduces the risk of a single accidental flag change placing a diagnostic model into production.[5]

### 12.3 Current Greenville v0.1 Status

The current Greenville artifact is reported to live at `model_registry/greenville_sc/hedonic_ppsf_v0_1.json`. It contains real trained Ridge values, an intercept of `5.2216`, full coefficient mappings, preprocessing notes, source and modeling dataset paths, status `diagnostic_only`, and `approved_for_recommendations: false`.[6]

| Item | Current Status |
|---|---|
| Corrected sale-date data available | Yes, for Greenville smoke test |
| Local regression pipeline runnable | Yes |
| Versioned coefficient artifact exists | Yes, per owner-reported Cursor summary |
| Registry validator exists | Yes, per owner-reported Cursor summary |
| Registry validation result | PASS, 11/11 checks, per owner-reported Cursor summary |
| Product approval | No |
| Recommendation use | Prohibited |

The current Greenville model must remain governed by this rule:[5]

```json
{
  "status": "diagnostic_only",
  "approved_for_recommendations": false
}
```

No application workflow should load this model for seller-facing ROI recommendations.

## 13. Model Promotion Gates

A model can pass structural validation and still fail promotion. The current Greenville v0.1 artifact is in exactly that category: it validates the artifact pattern, but it remains diagnostic-only.[5]

| Gate | Minimum Requirement Before Approval |
|---|---|
| Data validity | Correct `sale_date` coverage, documented source file, documented date window, sufficient usable rows |
| Reproducibility | Source dataset, modeling dataset, script path, run timestamp, and model artifact must be traceable |
| Structural validation | Registry validator must pass |
| Baseline metrics | R², MAE, RMSE, and preferably cross-validation metrics must be recorded |
| Residual diagnostics | Residuals should be reviewed by ZIP, price band, size band, time period, and other available segments |
| Coefficient sanity | Coefficient direction and scale must be reviewed for plausibility, especially when standardized features are used |
| Business usefulness | Model outputs must improve the tool’s ability to rank seller investments, not merely predict prices abstractly |
| Manual approval | A decision record must explicitly approve the artifact for recommendations |

Automatic approval is rejected. A future approval process must define who may set `approved_for_recommendations` to `true`, what evidence is required, and whether approval is market-specific, model-family-specific, or global.[4] [5]

## 14. Scarcity-Aware Seller Action Sequencer

The sequencer is central to the product mission because it determines not only what the seller should do, but **in what order** and under what timing constraints. The sequencer must account for available time before listing, action duration, lead time, dependencies, budget sequencing, carrying-cost impact, and anomaly warnings.

V4 preserves the V3/V11 position that the 13 previously missing duration/lead-time repair types are legitimate sequencer items and must not rely on generic fallback timing. The `duration_lead_time_rules` table remains a required MVP seed table.[3] [9]

| Sequencer Input | Source | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Repair/improvement catalog | Recommendation catalog | Must add parent-child support |
| Duration and lead-time ranges | Duration/Lead-Time Seed Table v1.0 | Draft complete for 13 rows, source QA open |
| Dependency ordering | Dependency Rules v1.0 Addendum and future full matrix export | Build blocker until row-exported and expert validated |
| Target list date | Seller input | Required for timeline feasibility and staging anomaly |
| Budget constraint | Seller input | Required for prioritization and phased plan generation |
| Carrying-cost assumptions | Product/financial assumptions | Must be explicit and source-labeled |
| Anomaly rules | Recommendation Anomaly Rules v1.0 | First MVP rule approved |
| ROI calibration input | Static assumptions or approved model artifacts only | Diagnostic models are prohibited |

## 15. Duration and Lead-Time Rules

The `duration_lead_time_rules` table must include explicit rows for the 13 previously missing repair types, plus subtype rows for accessibility. The broad accessibility row must be retained only for traceability and migration, not used for engine sequencing where subtype selection is available.[9]

| Field | Requirement |
|---|---|
| `rule_id` | Stable timing-rule identifier |
| `repair_type` | Canonical engine repair type or subtype |
| `parent_repair_type` | Nullable parent category; required for accessibility subtype rows |
| `tier` | MVP/phase tier from recommendation taxonomy |
| `duration_low_days` | Low-end duration estimate |
| `duration_high_days` | High-end duration estimate |
| `lead_time_low_days` | Low-end lead time; nullable only if pending source QA |
| `lead_time_high_days` | High-end lead time; nullable only if pending source QA |
| `source_reference` | Source citation, workpaper reference, or source-register ID |
| `review_status` | Controlled status such as `proposed_pending_source_QA`, `approved_by_owner`, `expert_validated`, or `deprecated` |
| `version` | Seed-table version |
| `decision_reference` | Governing decision ID |

The seed rows are conditionally usable for specification and data-shaping work, but should not be treated as final implementation values until row-level URLs, access dates, and review status are complete.[8] [9]

## 16. Accessibility Subtype Taxonomy

Decisions Log V11 approved splitting **Accessibility improvements** into at least two subtypes. This is not cosmetic; the subtypes have materially different duration, dependency, cost, and sequencing implications.[3]

| Parent Category | Engine Subtype | MVP Treatment | Sequencer Requirement |
|---|---|---|---|
| Accessibility improvements | Minor accessibility improvements | MVP | Use subtype-specific duration, lead time, dependency, cost, and trace output |
| Accessibility improvements | Exterior access modifications | MVP | Use subtype-specific duration, lead time, dependency, cost, and trace output |

The seller-facing UI may display a broader accessibility label where that improves comprehension, but the engine must store the subtype used in timing, sequencing, cost, and explanation logic. Remaining open work is to decide whether DOM impact should inherit from the parent accessibility category or split into subtype-specific DOM-impact rows.[10]

## 17. Dependency Rules

The dependency-rule family remains a build blocker. V11 resolves that the 13 missing duration rows are legitimate sequencer items and that accessibility must be subtype-aware, but it does not replace the need for a full row-level dependency matrix or expert validation.[11]

| Dependency Requirement | V4 Treatment |
|---|---|
| Full 30-type dependency matrix | Must be exported into executable `dependency_rules` seed rows |
| Expert validation | Required from a GC, remodeling PM, or equivalent reviewer before sequencer coding |
| Dependency type | Must distinguish hard, soft, none, and critical-path dependencies |
| Staging | Timing warning belongs in anomaly rules; dependency rules should only encode staging-before-photography or post-repair sequencing where supported |
| Landscaping / curb appeal | Must preserve dependency on disruptive exterior utility, grading, sewer, septic, or site work where present |
| Fixture replacement | Must preserve dependency on relevant rough plumbing/electrical work and inspection where applicable |
| Accessibility subtypes | Must not collapse exterior access modifications into minor grab-bar-level work |
| Model-calibrated ROI influence | Must not alter dependency ordering unless the model artifact is approved and traceable |

## 18. Recommendation Anomaly Rules

Anomaly rules are separate from ordinary ranking and scoring. They attach warnings or trace labels when an otherwise useful recommendation is constrained by timing, source quality, missing data, dependency uncertainty, model-approval status, or operational feasibility.[12]

The first approved MVP anomaly is professional staging under 21 days. The rule must fire when professional staging is recommended and the seller’s target list date is fewer than 21 calendar days away. This warning should not automatically suppress staging; it should mark the recommendation as a timeline risk and may influence urgency, constrained status, or beyond-timeline categorization depending on the rest of the scheduling calculation.[3] [12]

| Field | Requirement |
|---|---|
| `rule_id` | `ANOM-STAGING-UNDER-21-DAYS` |
| `repair_type` | Professional staging |
| `trigger_type` | `target_date_less_than_lead_time_high` or equivalent |
| `trigger_threshold_days` | `21` |
| `severity` | Timeline risk / scheduling risk |
| `message_template` | Draft copy pending UX/legal review |
| `decision_reference` | D-V11-002 |
| `version` | Anomaly rule version |

> **Draft staging warning copy:** Professional staging often requires advance scheduling. Because your target list date is less than 21 days away, staging may still be worthwhile, but availability could limit whether it can be completed before listing. Consider calling stagers immediately or using a faster partial-staging/photo-prep alternative.[12]

Model-artifact refusal should normally be handled as an engine guardrail rather than a seller-facing anomaly. If the seller-facing experience is affected because model-calibrated estimates are unavailable, the report should use confidence-qualified fallback language rather than exposing internal registry status.

## 19. Recommendation Output and Explanation Requirements

The output must explain why actions are recommended, ordered, delayed, warned, or omitted. A seller should be able to understand the rationale without needing to understand model internals.

| Output Element | Requirement |
|---|---|
| Priority rank | Show relative priority and category, not just raw score |
| Reason statement | Explain expected benefit, risk, and timing logic |
| Cost/value label | Show ranges or confidence-qualified estimates where exact values are unsupported |
| Timeline status | Identify urgent, feasible, constrained, beyond timeline, or not recommended |
| Dependency explanation | Show predecessor work where dependencies affect order |
| Anomaly callout | Show approved warnings such as staging under 21 days |
| Confidence label | Distinguish empirical, static assumption, owner-approved, expert-validated, placeholder, or approved-model basis |
| Model-use trace | Persist model ID and version internally if approved model calibration is used |
| Traceability | Persist internal trace IDs even if hidden from seller-facing report |

Seller-facing copy must not imply that diagnostic regression coefficients are valid value-uplift estimates. If the model path is not approved, the output must either rely on approved static assumptions or explain that some estimates are unavailable or confidence-limited.

## 20. Caching and Refresh Strategy

The system should minimize unnecessary external calls and preserve reproducibility of analysis runs. Runtime data should be cached with source timestamp and freshness metadata. Static datasets, seed tables, regression fixtures, and model artifacts should be versioned and refreshed through governed processes rather than ad hoc replacement.

| Data Type | Refresh / Cache Treatment |
|---|---|
| Property profile API results | Cache by property/source with retrieval timestamp and source version where available |
| ATTOM comparable-sales exports | Preserve source dataset path, export timestamp, mapping version, and date-window coverage |
| Regression fixtures | Treat known-good fixtures as immutable validation inputs unless explicitly superseded |
| Market datasets | Refresh on planned cadence defined in source register |
| Redfin or market pull data | Weekly ingestion if adopted; scheduler provider deferred |
| Seed tables | Versioned releases; no silent overwrite of production seed data |
| Model-registry artifacts | Versioned artifacts; no silent overwrite; promotion status changes require decision trace |
| Generated reports | Store immutable report snapshot linked to analysis run, seed versions, and model versions where applicable |
| Source workpapers | Preserve as evidence artifacts; update through source-register versioning |

Decisions Log V12 rejects immediate full 12-metro repulls for now. Additional ATTOM usage should wait until Greenville diagnostics clarify whether weak performance is caused by missing features, noisy geography, insufficient row count, target choice, or other data-quality issues.[4] [7]

## 21. Security, Privacy, and Access Control

The system must protect seller information, property data, API credentials, generated reports, model artifacts, diagnostics, and source workpapers. Secrets must remain server-side. Reports and uploaded files must not be public by default.

| Security Area | Requirement |
|---|---|
| Secrets | Store server-side; use managed vault for production where feasible |
| API keys | Never expose in frontend; route calls through backend adapters |
| Object storage | Private by default with backend-generated short-lived signed access URLs |
| Authentication | Required if persistent user accounts, saved reports, or private files are included |
| Authorization | Users may access only their own properties, analyses, and reports unless sharing is explicitly implemented |
| Model artifacts | Restrict write access; approval flags must not be user-editable through public flows |
| Audit logging | Record significant recommendation runs, source versions, seed versions, model versions, approval states, and report generation events |
| Data minimization | Do not collect unnecessary personally identifiable or sensitive seller data |

## 22. Legal, Compliance, and Claims Controls

The product must not present outputs as appraisals, legal advice, tax advice, contractor guarantees, or guaranteed resale outcomes. The recommendation engine should use careful language around estimated value, likely buyer perception, timeline risk, source confidence, and model maturity.

| Legal Risk | Control |
|---|---|
| Appraisal-like claims | Use estimate/confidence language and disclaimers; avoid guaranteed value language |
| Diagnostic model misuse | Prohibit diagnostic coefficients in seller-facing recommendations and trace all approved model use |
| Contractor timeline certainty | Present duration/lead-time ranges and scheduling risk labels |
| Repair safety or code compliance | Recommend professional review where safety, permitting, electrical, plumbing, structural, or access modifications are involved |
| Fair housing / demographic misuse | Avoid protected-class targeting or discriminatory recommendation logic |
| Multi-state release risk | Conduct legal review before national public release |
| Source licensing | Confirm rights and terms for any downloaded or API-derived data before production use |

## 23. Testing Strategy

Testing must validate correctness, traceability, source governance, model-artifact governance, and user-facing explanation quality. Engine tests should be deterministic and should verify that seed-table version changes and model-version changes produce expected trace changes.

| Test Category | Required Coverage |
|---|---|
| Unit tests | Scoring components, timeline feasibility, anomaly triggers, dependency resolution, confidence labels |
| Seed-table validation | Required fields, nullability rules, review-status vocabulary, source references, version consistency |
| ATTOM mapping tests | Validate approved `sale_date` fallback order and reject reliance on defective path |
| Regression/calibration tests | Missingness, sign, magnitude, coefficient bounds, data leakage, sensitivity |
| Model-registry validation tests | Required fields, training-window fields, metrics presence, coefficient/feature-name matching, local path existence, diagnostic-only approval-flag rule |
| Promotion-readiness tests | Ensure structurally valid models are not automatically approved and promotion criteria are reported separately |
| Recommendation-engine refusal tests | Verify diagnostic, candidate, retired, rejected, structurally invalid, and `approved_for_recommendations: false` artifacts are refused |
| Integration tests | External adapters, source-status logging, cache behavior, graceful degradation |
| End-to-end tests | Full seller workflow from intake through report generation |
| Report-output review | Explanation clarity, warning visibility, disclaimer presence, anomaly copy, confidence language |
| Security tests | Authorization, object access, secret exposure, signed URL expiry, model-artifact write controls |
| Governance tests | Analysis trace includes seed versions, source IDs, coefficient/model IDs, approval flags, and decision references where applicable |

The owner-reported model-registry validator passed 11 of 11 checks against the Greenville v0.1 artifact. This validates the artifact contract but does not approve the model. Future automated tests must preserve that distinction.[6]

## 24. Observability and Auditability

The system should record enough metadata to reproduce recommendation outputs and diagnose model, seed, or source issues. This is especially important because MVP recommendations may rely partly on static assumptions and because current regression artifacts remain diagnostic-only.

| Audit Item | Required Trace |
|---|---|
| Analysis run | Input snapshot, timestamp, user/property ID, engine version |
| Recommendation | Candidate ID, final rank, score components, category, explanation |
| Timing | Duration/lead-time rule ID, version, source status |
| Dependency | Dependency-rule IDs and critical-path flags applied |
| Anomaly | Anomaly IDs triggered and message templates used |
| Source | Source-register IDs, access dates, confidence labels |
| ATTOM comparable export | Mapping version, source dataset path, export timestamp, sale-date coverage |
| Model artifact | Model ID, version, market, target, status, approval flag, validation report path |
| Coefficients | Coefficient-set version, calibration status, placeholder/empirical/model flag |
| Report | Generated report version and output timestamp |

