## 27. MVP / Phase 2 / Phase 3 / Reject Classification

V4 keeps the project focused on the smallest valuable product that can answer the seller’s core question.

| Capability | Classification | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Guided seller/property intake | MVP | Required to produce seller-specific plan |
| Recommendation catalog with parent-child subtype support | MVP | Required by accessibility split and traceability |
| Duration/lead-time rules | MVP | Required for sequencing and timeline feasibility |
| Full dependency rules | MVP | Required to answer “in what order” |
| Professional staging anomaly | MVP | Approved by V11 and directly supports timeline guidance |
| Source registry and seed versioning | MVP | Required for defensibility and maintenance |
| ATTOM corrected `sale_date` mapping | MVP | Required for valid comparable-sales regression fixtures |
| Greenville corrected smoke-test fixture | MVP / diagnostic | Required for local pipeline validation and low-cost diagnostics |
| Model registry artifact pattern | MVP architecture | Required to keep coefficients out of code and diagnostics-only outputs |
| Model-registry validation | MVP governance | Required before any artifact can even be considered structurally safe |
| Promotion-readiness diagnostics | MVP | Required before any coefficient artifact can be approved |
| Recommendation-engine refusal of unapproved artifacts | MVP | Required to protect seller-facing flows from diagnostic coefficients |
| Regression calibration with observed fields only | MVP / readiness-dependent | Required for credible ROI logic, but constrained by data availability and promotion gates |
| Full 12-metro repull | Defer within MVP | Rejected for now until Greenville diagnostics justify broader API usage |
| Live contractor scheduling | Phase 2 or Phase 3 | High complexity and maintenance burden |
| Database-backed model registry | Phase 2 / Phase 3 | Useful for production hardening but not required for diagnostic workflow |
| Automated permit/code determination | Phase 3 | High legal and jurisdictional complexity |
| Exact resale-value guarantee | Reject | Unsupported and legally risky |
| Generic fallback timing for sequencer-required rows | Reject for MVP sequencer | V11 explicitly requires explicit coverage |
| Seller-facing use of Greenville v0.1 diagnostic coefficients | Reject | V12 explicitly does not approve the coefficients |
| Automatic model approval | Reject | V12 requires validation plus future promotion-readiness and manual approval |

## 28. Implementation Sequence Recommendation

The next step after V4 should not be broad code generation or seller-facing coefficient wiring. The project should close the data, governance, diagnostic, and sequencer gates that control the recommendation engine.

| Sequence | Artifact / Workstream | Outcome |
|---:|---|---|
| 1 | Confirm Technical Specification V4 as the governing technical spec | Shared implementation-control baseline |
| 2 | Preserve corrected ATTOM `sale_date` mapper requirements in API/data-adapter specification | Future pulls retain valid sale-date data |
| 3 | Keep Greenville v0.1 as `diagnostic_only` and `approved_for_recommendations: false` | Safe model-governance posture |
| 4 | Run Greenville diagnostics and promotion-readiness reporting | Evidence for missing features, target choice, residual bias, and expansion decision |
| 5 | Define model-promotion thresholds and manual approval authority | Prevent accidental or subjective promotion |
| 6 | Add recommendation-engine refusal tests for unapproved model artifacts | Ensures safe failure behavior before integration |
| 7 | Static Data Source Register V2 full update | Authoritative source inventory and provenance model |
| 8 | Duration/lead-time row-level source QA | Final executable timing seed table |
| 9 | Recommendation catalog taxonomy alignment | Consistent repair/subtype IDs across artifacts |
| 10 | Full dependency matrix export | Executable dependency seed rows |
| 11 | Dependency expert validation | Sequencer implementation readiness |
| 12 | Recommendation Engine Specification | Precise scoring, ranking, sequencing, anomaly, model-use, and explanation logic |
| 13 | Testing Plan | Acceptance criteria and regression suite before coding |
| 14 | Implementation planning | Code only after the affected specification gates are closed |

